# Line endings are a packaging concern here, not a style preference.
#
# `bin/leastgrant.js` starts with `#!/usr/bin/env node`. If that line ends CRLF,
# the kernel looks for an interpreter called `node\r` and the CLI fails to start
# on Linux and macOS with a message that names a program nobody has heard of.
#
# Git on Windows defaults to `core.autocrlf=true`, so without this file a fresh
# clone on a Windows machine gets CRLF in the working tree, and `npm pack` packs
# exactly what is in the working tree. A release cut from Windows would ship a
# broken executable.
#
# It also matters for the release pipeline's central claim. That pipeline proves
# "what was published is what was verified" by comparing the sha512 of the
# tarball it built against the registry's `dist.integrity`. Two machines can
# only produce the same bytes if they agree about line endings.
#
# `eol=lf` rather than plain `text=auto`: the latter normalises what goes *into*
# git but still hands Windows a CRLF working tree.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Things that must never be touched.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.tgz binary
*.gz binary
